  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-7}-1}{x-4}=$",
  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-7}-1}{x-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(4)-7}-1}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-7}-1}{x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-7}-1)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x-7})}^{2}-{1}^{2}}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{2x-7-1}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{2x-8}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{2(x-4)}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x-7}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(4)-7}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
